let's talk about covid in China  the government spent the last three  years enforcing a super strict policy  known as zero covet  then basically overnight  China abandoned almost all of its  restrictions this was done in a sort of  convulsive political Lurch people were  not prepared for it so many hospitals  are completely under strained medical  staff under immense pressure  crematoriums are working overtime and  now it looks like it's going to get even  worse with so many people on the move of  a Lunar New Year holiday millions of  workers are expected to travel from  cities back to their rural home towns so  many people have been kept away for so  long from seeing relatives and things  like that they're expecting a very very  large migration so why did China make  this sudden u-turn on covid what could  this huge wave of infections mean for  the country and how will it affect the  rest of the world  so since the early days of the pandemic  China was living under this stringent  zero coveted policy which meant  basically that the government considered  one infection one infection too many  people with covid and their close  contacts were forced into state-run  quarantine camps cities and regions were  in and out of lockdowns and you'd get  things like an entire apartment block  being quarantined because of just one  case  domestic and international travel was  heavily restricted too  and it seems that for a long time people  generally supported this approach the  zero covered policy in China was  especially at the beginning of the  pandemic a source of national pride here  in China the official death toll at  least was very low but as the rest of  the world started to loosen restrictions  and open up the Chinese government  didn't budge  people were getting angry there were  reports of things like people being  locked inside a building during an  earthquake  another is unable to escape a burning  apartment block because of lockdown  measures something Chinese officials  denied  that fire in the city of arimchib which  is in the West Was a big moment because  it triggered public protests against  zero covid which then spread to many of  the big cities across the country  protests like this are super rare  [Music]  suddenly in early December it was all  over the government changed its policy  from an all-out war on covid to calling  it a mild disease and relaxing the rules  we saw them charging as hard as possible  in the direction of zerocovid and now  we're seeing them charged as hard as  possible in the direction of  implementation of dismantling zero  covered some people say it was the  protests that spooked the leadership  into changing course and the added  political pressure for sure but also the  economic and social costs were just  getting so high and trying to contain  the Omicron variant was like fighting a  losing battle because it's way more  infectious than the original strains but  I think the government was looking for  some sort of way out because they backed  themselves into a corner so where are we  now well covert has already ripped  through China's major cities like  Beijing and Shanghai it literally went  from me not knowing anybody who called  Corbett in China  to  everybody catching literally everybody  catching covert in a space of a few  weeks it was really surreal the problem  is there's been no consistent reporting  of data on the number of cases or deaths  and the government stopped publishing  daily figures in late December we do  have a couple of key numbers though  according to leaked reports from  government meetings China's officials  reckon that 250 million people got  covert during the first 20 days of  December that's double the population of  Japan in 20 days more recently a health  official announced that almost 60  thousand people died from covid over  five weeks and that the numbers were now  dropping Parliament  the World Health Organization has been  asking China for better data and says it  welcomes these latest figures to help  understand the situation although it  would still like to see a more detailed  breakdown  now whether or not zero covid was the  right call whether it lasted too long  those are ongoing debates but wherever  you stand on that a lot of people are  questioning why there wasn't a better  exit strategy China bought itself time  for the virus to become milder but you  would also think that in buying itself  time over those three years that it  would have made basic preparations but  what we saw when the virus restrictions  were lifted we saw a very different  picture we saw chaos and essentially  millions of people defenseless in the  face of this virus and that's where  China's vaccine program comes in for  criticism most people have had two doses  of a chinese-made vaccine but Studies  have shown they aren't quite as  effective as some of the western ones  although they still offer some  protection against severe disease and  death the bigger problem is that not  enough people have had third or fourth  doses especially the most vulnerable  the government took their foot off the  gas about over a year ago fall in terms  of getting people to do more booster  shots and so people early on who might  have been protected from earlier  versions of covid aren't getting  protection offered by newer boosters or  newer Doses and now we've got the Lunar  New Year  [Applause]  when huge numbers of people travel from  the cities to rural areas we're talking  two billion Trips Trips that many  haven't been able to make since the  pandemic started  and all that travel is expected to  create another surge of covet infections  in rural communities that are even less  prepared the healthcare capacity is  there are even weaker and the  vaccination levels in the rural areas  are also much weaker so there's a lot of  concern that this is going to do more  damage there are projections that we  could be looking at around 2 million  um dead in China by the time this has  has played out in some ways it's a  really scary picture but there's another  side to all of this the many who are  young and healthy who may have caught  the virus already especially in the  bigger cities I have to say there is  actually a lot of optimism and a lot of  positivity going forward I think people  are more relieved than anything that  they can just live normal lives and that  China will at least start to be  reconnected with the rest of the world  so what does all this mean for the rest  of the world well on the health front  there's a concern that what's happening  in China could somehow increase the risk  of a dangerous new variant emerging so  far though based on the genetic data  provided by the Chinese government at  least there's no sign of that anytime  you have spread at a massive scale it  does allow for the opportunity for the  virus to evolve that's simply a product  of the virus being replicated and spread  from person to person at a massive scale  and although it's possible that that can  happen and may have happened the  dominant streams being reported are  versions of Omicron that we already have  and so the relative risk to other  regions of the world is pretty low then  the other big question is about how all  of this affects the global economy  foreign  took a huge hit during the pandemic last  year growth dropped to its lowest level  in 40 years people lost jobs factories  shut down and many foreign companies got  nervous about investing in China because  things were so unpredictable especially  with all the stop-start lockdowns  [Music]  it's reopening the economy is expected  to pick up how quickly that'll happen is  what a lot of people are wondering hope  and the expectation on the Chinese  government's part is that once this has  rippled out later in the year you'll  have a kind of bounce back that's good  news for people in China and the  countries and industries that rely  heavily on doing business with China  there's a saying that when China sneezes  the global economy catches a cold China  is such a huge engine for powering the  global economy one example of how zero  covert affected the global economy is  that it basically choked so many of  these Global Supply chains and all of a  sudden  people were having difficulties sourcing  things as basic as a paper cup but a  side effect of China's recovery might be  that it makes Global inflation worse  than it already is as Chinese factories  and businesses get going again they'll  need to import more Commodities like oil  and iron ore that increases demand and  pushes up their price which can then  have a knock-on effect on the price of  so many other things we buy  so everything that's going on in China  right now really brings home how far  reaching the effects of the pandemic are  and it's a painful reminder that this  thing's not over yet  check out our last episode about the  protests in Iran if you want to  understand what's going on there and let  us know in the comments what other  topics you want us to explain  [Music]
 

